113
00:09:48,880 --> 00:09:52,320
That I think and I just I enjoy the process of making apps.

114
00:09:52,480 --> 00:10:05,680
And so it's that it's very much that kind of thing of I always like the dream was always that thought of I could if I can make just enough money on a day to day basis from my apps that I can stop taking on consulting.

115
00:10:05,840 --> 00:10:06,640
I never liked consulting.

116
00:10:06,720 --> 00:10:07,760
That was just a means to an end.

117
00:10:07,920 --> 00:10:08,000
Right.

118
00:10:08,320 --> 00:10:10,320
And I can then just sort of make the things that I like.

119
00:10:10,640 --> 00:10:16,240
And I like the lifestyle and the kind of feeling of being an independent developer.

120
00:10:16,400 --> 00:10:17,360
And so that was always the dream.

121
00:10:17,440 --> 00:10:29,600
And it's like for those early days, I remember kind of backing out a calculation of, okay, what is like the bare minimum salary that I need on a yearly basis to just be fine?

122
00:10:30,640 --> 00:10:40,720
And then I took that and then like work out our expenses and our taxes and health insurance and all the things that I have to kind of like add on to that and then work it out back to how much do I need to make in the App Store per day?

123
00:10:41,120 --> 00:11:25,840
And it was just like I had a number that was I every like every Monday I ran a report for iTunes Connect and I would see how close I was getting to that because that was sort of in my mind my goal was it's like I needed to make whatever it was I can't even remember it was like maybe like $140 a day or something in the app store then like if I could do that it wouldn't be a great living but it would be enough that I could like make ends meet and so that was always the goal and it was just kind of this process of you know I I mean I launched countless apps to get to that point kept trying and thinking of new ideas or and this was certainly in the period where they were just if you actually had an idea for an app you never knew if it was going to be a hit you never knew because it's like it's a like it's a brand new app concept.

124
00:11:26,400 --> 00:11:29,360
It's in the situation you have now where there's such saturation in the app store.

125
00:11:29,680 --> 00:11:29,840
Right.

126
00:11:30,080 --> 00:11:48,480
And so at that point it was very different where it's like, you know, I was one of the first, well, I definitely wrote the first Per Diamond reference app because it's like, I don't think there's a particularly broad market for that, but even like in more developed categories, there are a lot of areas where it's like there's opportunities to be the first or one of the first.

127
00:11:49,840 --> 00:11:58,960
And so it was just kind of continuing, my initial approach was just to try and maximize the number of opportunities I had by continuing to hit as many new categories as I could.

128
00:11:59,280 --> 00:11:59,360
Yeah.

129
00:12:00,640 --> 00:12:06,960
That calculation, I feel like I know multiple people who are sort of in that process now or who were recently in that process.

130
00:12:07,440 --> 00:12:26,480
And that was, to me, thinking about that situation was exactly what was so exciting about Apple dropping their cut for people who make less than a million dollars, because like that 15%, I feel like dramatically would have bumped you forward and when you could have gone totally full-time.

131
00:12:26,560 --> 00:12:27,200
Oh, absolutely.

132
00:12:27,840 --> 00:12:29,680
By a significant time period.

133
00:12:30,880 --> 00:12:36,560
And like, maybe I'm, because I haven't, you know, crossed that threshold, but it feels.

134
00:12:37,120 --> 00:12:51,600
like once you do cross that threshold and then you can spend all of your time working on the apps, the actual quality of your output, whether it's the number of apps or the app that you're working on getting more feature rich or whatever, can kind of balloon.

135
00:12:52,160 --> 00:12:53,760
And that can grow a lot faster after that, right?

136
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:59,680
Yeah, no, I feel like there is definitely a compounding effect once you're able to...

137
00:13:00,560 --> 00:13:02,560
focus exclusively or primarily.

138
00:13:03,200 --> 00:13:11,920
You know, it's like I've always had other things like I have a podcast that I do and so like that's a side thing to my apps now, but the primary focus is what I do is working on the apps.

139
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:23,760
And I think once you're able to give that your primary attention rather than whatever your side thing is, your day job, your consulting work, whatever that might be, it compounds on itself.

140
00:13:24,080 --> 00:13:45,600
And in a weird way, it creates this slight sense of at least I found it was this sense of accountability and I put myself more into it because now my livelihood is writing on this and I can take it more seriously and that taking it by taking it seriously, it also allows me to do better work because I'm not cutting corners.

141
00:13:45,680 --> 00:13:46,560
I'm not being silly.

142
00:13:46,960 --> 00:13:54,720
It is something that requires serious attention because that's what's ultimately going to be paying my mortgage and you know, buying my groceries.

143
00:13:55,200 --> 00:14:07,120
And so I think there's definitely like the closer you can get and the quicker you can get to that point, there's definitely this feeling where it's like it's an acceleration, like there's an inflection point that happens that accelerates your work.

144
00:14:07,360 --> 00:14:09,440
And I think definitely to your point with the small business program.

145
00:14:09,760 --> 00:14:21,280
It's very cool to me because when I think back to those days, it would have been, I mean, it's hard to know exactly, but it would have been several months, I think, earlier that I could have stopped, you know, like one or two more contracts that I never would have had to take.

146
00:14:22,160 --> 00:14:24,880
And so I would have started that compounding effect that much earlier.

147
00:14:25,200 --> 00:14:33,680
So I think it's super exciting that it's like Apple is doing that for the developers, maybe if I said other it is, for almost every developer they're doing this.

148
00:14:33,840 --> 00:14:42,800
