149
00:14:43,200 --> 00:15:00,640
It makes me wonder if in the next year we'll see a little bit of a bump in sort of the indie highly craft apps because it's more viable for more of those people to switch from doing it, you know, like you said, as a side project to something where they can really think.

150
00:15:00,720 --> 00:15:03,360
really throw all their full time and attention to it.

151
00:15:03,680 --> 00:15:05,520
Yeah, no, I think it'll be very interesting to see.

152
00:15:05,920 --> 00:15:06,000
Yeah.

153
00:15:06,720 --> 00:15:26,960
So the other thing you talked about, which I feel like as I entered this world and became kind of obsessed with your podcast under the radar, by the way, I've always had is like your sort of strategy seemed to be what you mentioned earlier, which is try lots of things.

154
00:15:27,520 --> 00:15:36,240
It seems like a lot of the well-known indie app developers in this space kind of put all their eggs in one basket and spend a whole bunch of time and kind of release this.

155
00:15:36,320 --> 00:16:21,120
this big thing to the world and hope that it catches on and if it doesn't they'll try another one but yours seems to be a much quicker turnaround with way more apps that you were trying especially early on what was like what kind of led you to to doing it that way i mean i suppose in some ways you could say that um a lack of success drove me to to need to do that that you know like if the first app i had launched had been wildly successful i now i would have necessarily ended up on this path uh because i wouldn't have needed to but it um you know as i was going i found that i was able to get enough traction with the apps I was having that it's like they were layering on top of each other enough, you know, sort of building towards that income point that I was hoping.

156
00:16:22,080 --> 00:16:30,240
But never I never had any it took a long time before I had any one app that was sort of making a significant income for me.

157
00:16:30,480 --> 00:16:32,480
And so I kind of that's how I got started.

158
00:16:32,640 --> 00:16:36,720
And then once that was sort of the pattern I got into, I just started also discovering that I really enjoyed it.

159
00:16:37,600 --> 00:16:55,520
And I one of the, you know, the favorite, my favorite part of this job is that first like maybe 40% of developing an app where you're it's all fresh and it's all interesting and new and you're solving all the cool problems and you're not having to deal with some of the back end or the marketing stuff that I don't enjoy as much.

160
00:16:55,760 --> 00:16:57,040
And I really love that process.

161
00:16:57,760 --> 00:17:02,800
And so once I got a taste of like, well, like, gee, I can just keep doing this and it's fine.

162
00:17:03,840 --> 00:17:04,960
I kind of got into that habit.

163
00:17:05,200 --> 00:17:12,160
And I think I'm up to, gosh, I think it's something like 57 apps or 56 apps at this point that I've launched over 12 years.

164
00:17:12,640 --> 00:17:14,480
And so it's, you know, it's certainly a pattern.

165
00:17:14,560 --> 00:17:16,160
I think the pace is certainly slowed down.

166
00:17:16,320 --> 00:17:26,240
And I think some of that is just obviously once I've had some apps that had some traction, had some success, there was an obvious period where I would reinvest into those apps.

167
00:17:26,640 --> 00:17:26,800
Right.

168
00:17:27,200 --> 00:17:40,480
And make them better and more developed and kind of flesh them out before I move on to the next step versus in the earlier periods where I've launched many apps that have gone absolutely nowhere that have had like two downloads and then like that's it.

169
00:17:40,640 --> 00:17:43,440
And so for those apps, they never got a version 1.1.

