177
00:18:13,200 --> 00:18:24,480
Before I know you've talked about this on your shows before, how like having a profile in this community is helpful for the initial launch, but once you once you're in the store, you sort of, you're like everyone else.

178
00:18:24,560 --> 00:18:26,960
You're just trying to get attention of people who don't already know who you are.

179
00:18:27,120 --> 00:18:27,200
Yeah.

180
00:18:27,760 --> 00:18:32,000
But especially early on, you didn't have this big profile or anything like you kind of have now.

181
00:18:32,640 --> 00:18:34,640
How did you try to get attention in the store?

182
00:18:35,600 --> 00:18:35,680
Sure.

183
00:18:36,240 --> 00:18:57,600
I mean, I think so I think the early days were like trying to do my best to be like a student of the App Store and understand what I could do in there to, you know, like I was saying with being, you know, updating your app frequently so you're in the recently changed or what screenshots work well, which screenshots don't, having good marketing text in the App Store.

184
00:18:57,840 --> 00:19:20,240
And then, I mean, a lot, a lot beyond that, it's just, it was, it was sort of intentionally trying my best to develop, it's the way they say it's like, but to develop a little bit of a platform to be able to broadcast out to and that largely the obvious place for that ended up being in the iOS developer community which is sort of helpful and sort of not helpful.

185
00:19:20,640 --> 00:20:46,960
It's helpful because obviously it's an area that I'm an expert in and so I can I can speak with authority in a way that's authentic and so having it makes sense that that's a place that people would come and sort of have a you know care what I say whereas if I wanted to try and develop an audience in like cooking or something I have no expertise I have no specialty there and so it's like I would have been really useful when I made a recipe organizer if I had an audience in the cooking space but I I have no had no such thing and so that never really worked worked out and then I mean beyond that it has just been huge amounts of cold calls and emails kind of thing and just continuing I mean I think in many ways I don't really I don't really feel like I'm very good at marketing I think it's something that I do well enough and I kind of fall back on a few basic things of you know reaching out to a few people in the press and trying to have reasonable screenshots and trying to have reasonable abstract optimization kind of things and just sort of hoping for the best and it's I mean, I guess in some ways I just hope that the thing that I am actually good at, the thing which is, you know, like the actual programming side, can in some ways make up for a little bit of my lack of ability or challenge in the marketing side, which make the apps hopefully in some ways like, you know, the best kind of marketing that for an independent developer, I think is kind of that word-to-mouth based marketing.

186
00:20:47,360 --> 00:20:52,240
And so that's the thing that I can do by being trying to make the app nice, I guess.

187
00:20:52,880 --> 00:20:59,360
But the actual, like, formal marketing or like, any time I've tried to do, like, paid advertising or things, I have no idea what I'm doing.

188
00:20:59,600 --> 00:21:00,640
And I feel like I'm almost.

189
00:21:01,120 --> 00:21:05,760
always just like, I'm just throwing money down, you know, down the drain, um, whenever I try it.

190
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:06,080
Yeah.

191
00:21:06,240 --> 00:21:19,680
They say you have to like spend, I, I, you know, everyone has a different, probably totally random number, but it's always astronomical how much money people will basically flush down the toilet to learn how to advertise, uh, correctly.

192
00:21:19,920 --> 00:21:20,000
Yeah.

193
00:21:20,240 --> 00:21:23,120
And they're like, you're not going to actually start making money until you've spent this much.

194
00:21:23,280 --> 00:21:25,920
And I'm like, whoa, I don't know if I'll ever get there.

195
00:21:26,720 --> 00:21:26,800
Yeah.

196
00:21:27,200 --> 00:21:36,880
No, I think that, and it's, it's the reality, I think it's the understanding that I have that, like, there are people who, you know, to your question at the beginning, like, did you, did I do education?

197
00:21:37,600 --> 00:21:39,520
around what I'm ultimately doing?

198
00:21:39,680 --> 00:21:47,200
It's like there are people who go to school to learn about marketing and to learn about advertising and then go and work in a advertising company and a PR firm.

199
00:21:47,600 --> 00:22:01,760
And it's an industry and an expertise that I'm not going to be, I shouldn't be able to expect to just that I'm just going to be able to show up and do a professional amazing job based on some medium posts or something.

200
00:22:02,400 --> 00:22:02,720
Exactly.

201
00:22:02,880 --> 00:22:04,480
I mean, you're not too far from it.

202
00:22:04,640 --> 00:22:11,040
Like it is a lot of medium posts and seeing what someone else does and then trying it out myself with my own spin and that kind of thing.

203
00:22:11,040 --> 00:22:12,640
But it's it's certainly never going to be at the level.

204
00:22:12,640 --> 00:22:17,440
They're definitely never going to be at the level of someone who that's their job, that's their expertise, that's their speciality.

205
00:22:17,920 --> 00:22:27,360
They've spent someone else's money on advertising to such a degree that they know what they're doing and can't just burn through a bunch of money without getting any result.

206
00:22:28,240 --> 00:22:32,240
Yeah, that makes sense and that jives with my experience.

207
00:22:32,640 --> 00:22:45,680
One thing that is really nice though is that the iOS community, like on Twitter, seems to be very open to sharing all the experiments that they do, or at least the groups that I've kind of found.

208
00:22:46,880 --> 00:22:48,400
And that does feel like that's helpful.

209
00:22:48,880 --> 00:22:53,040
Although most of the time it's kind of like I spent this much and tried this and then it didn't work.

210
00:22:53,600 --> 00:22:56,320
And so it usually makes me scared to even try it.

211
00:22:56,480 --> 00:22:58,080
But yeah.

212
00:22:58,400 --> 00:22:59,200
Yeah, I guess it is.

213
00:22:59,440 --> 00:23:03,840
Like you'd have to really dive in and choose that as a skill you want to build up.

214
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:07,760
It's not something you're just going to just kind of try one day.

215
00:23:08,240 --> 00:23:11,600
Like I want to learn how to use the new collection view layout.

216
00:23:13,120 --> 00:23:16,400
It's not a skill that you can just build off of skills that you already have.

217
00:23:16,560 --> 00:23:17,440
It's a totally new thing.

218
00:23:17,680 --> 00:23:17,760
Yeah.

219
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:20,960
And I think it's a speciality like anything else.

220
00:23:21,040 --> 00:23:22,560
And I do think I appreciate that.

221
00:23:23,200 --> 00:23:35,520
I think our community is broadly fairly generous with sharing that kind of stuff because in many ways it's sort of orthogonal to what our actual, like, we're not competing against each other in that same way.

222
00:23:35,680 --> 00:23:38,400
Like, we're all making our own apps in our own areas.

223
00:23:39,440 --> 00:23:42,800
And it there's a little bit of, you know, it's like we all benefit from it.

224
00:23:42,960 --> 00:23:53,200
And so it's been the way since the beginning of the iOS sort of dev community has always been very sort of welcoming and helpful and sort of supportive of each other.

225
00:23:53,440 --> 00:24:10,320
I feel like that even when it became that, you know, the biggest apps were from big companies, there still has always been this kind of core ethos of a group of iOS developers that are much more in it just to support and encourage and sort of help other people make apps.

226
00:24:11,360 --> 00:24:15,200
Now, early on, you were not exclusive to iOS, right?

227
00:24:15,760 --> 00:24:19,520
You experimented on the Google, I guess it used to be Google Android market?

228
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:22,240
Yeah, so I mean, I've tried that.

229
00:24:22,400 --> 00:24:25,040
I mean, I even launched an app on Windows Phone at one point.

230
00:24:26,560 --> 00:24:27,280
You're the other one.

231
00:24:28,080 --> 00:24:36,960
It's like, there was a, there was that period where I was just, I didn't care too much about what, it's like, isn't it?

232
00:24:36,960 --> 00:24:46,400
It's not like I didn't care what I was making, but it's like my goal was just to make find that way to make that path where I could be like a sustainable income from apps.

233
00:24:46,560 --> 00:24:48,160
And so I tried a bunch of that.

234
00:24:48,320 --> 00:24:50,880
I never really had much luck or success at all there.

235
00:24:51,120 --> 00:24:58,320
And so it never really went far, but I've certainly launched apps on a couple of different platforms just to see and experiment.

236
00:24:58,800 --> 00:25:05,280
And especially in the period where they were like, you know, and like Amazon launched their app store on Google.

237
00:25:05,600 --> 00:25:07,680
And it was like, I don't know, is this going to be a big deal?

238
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:09,360
Maybe it is, maybe it isn't.

239
00:25:09,360 --> 00:25:11,920
And so like being there on day one, like maybe that's a cool thing.

240
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:13,520
And so I tried, you know, I did that.

241
00:25:13,600 --> 00:25:14,240
It didn't go anywhere.

242
00:25:14,480 --> 00:25:18,720
You know, I never really got any downloads from that app store, but there was a period where I tried that.

243
00:25:18,960 --> 00:25:24,080
But I mean, I think I always was like, I'm not a particularly old Mac guy.

244
00:25:24,240 --> 00:25:31,680
Like, it was, you know, I think many people in this community were Mac developers before they were iOS developers, um, at least if, you know, having, if you've been doing it as long as I have.

245
00:25:31,920 --> 00:25:40,080
But my, you know, my background, um, has nothing to do, you know, I was a PC guy for the majority of my, you know, education, uh, sort of period.

246
00:25:40,320 --> 00:25:44,880
And I only got a Mac because I wanted to get Textmate when I was a Ruby on Rails developer.

247
00:25:45,040 --> 00:25:48,800
And like Textmate was the, was the IDE that everyone used essentially.

248
00:25:49,840 --> 00:25:50,800
And so I needed to get a Mac.

249
00:25:51,040 --> 00:25:52,640
And so I bought a Mac so I could run TextMate.

250
00:25:53,040 --> 00:25:54,880
And like I had never used a Mac before.

251
00:25:54,960 --> 00:25:58,080
I didn't really like, have this affinity for it or this thing.

252
00:25:58,240 --> 00:26:03,360
It was just a tool that I got and it turned out working out well because then, you know, it's like, become an app developer later.

253
00:26:04,160 --> 00:26:07,600
But I didn't come, like I certainly have grown to have a tremendous affection for Apple.

254
00:26:07,920 --> 00:26:12,640
But at the time, it wasn't like I was coming at it as a, like, a diehard, you know, Apple fanboy.

255
00:26:12,800 --> 00:26:12,960
Right.

256
00:26:13,040 --> 00:26:15,760
There was no, like, loyalty to the brand early on.

257
00:26:16,000 --> 00:26:16,080
Yeah.

258
00:26:16,400 --> 00:26:16,880
That makes sense.

259
00:26:17,040 --> 00:27:01,520
No, no, I think it's certainly, yeah, it's like, early, early on it was, I mean, I enjoyed it and I liked it, but it was not something that I had that sort of this lifelong commitment to that I certainly if you imagine a company like the Icon Factory or Panic or some of these really old bare bones like there's some of these very old like established Mac companies that have been here forever and so when they came into iOS it's from a very different perspective than someone who just sort of you know I bought my first Mac probably the year before I started working on app development and so it was you know it was a very recent thing for me so you had been doing like iOS development for a little while I think you even had switched over to fully independent by this point but then you you were just trying a lot of things.

260
00:27:01,680 --> 00:27:08,400
Like you had a kind of a successful audiobook kind of app and you had a recreate book app.

261
00:27:09,120 --> 00:27:15,840
But at some point you started specializing a little bit in health apps and Apple Watch apps.

262
00:27:16,480 --> 00:27:21,120
And one thing that I'm where I came in in this timeline, I'm not sure which one of those kind came first.

263
00:27:23,040 --> 00:27:25,120
So the first app I made that was a health and fitness app.

264
00:27:25,280 --> 00:27:36,320
And this was the app that I feel like has, I mean, until Widgetsmith, I would say it was heads and shoulders, the most successful kind of app I ever made was an app called Pedometer plus plus, which was a step counting app for iOS.

265
00:27:36,800 --> 00:27:51,120
And so I introduced this with the iPhone 5S, it was when Apple added step counting capabilities to the, you know, sort of to the phone, but they didn't ship any way of doing this in a first party way.

266
00:27:51,280 --> 00:27:58,160
So like this capability existed, but it was entirely a third party opportunity and I was the first person to write an app to take advantage of it.

267
00:27:58,560 --> 00:28:00,320
And they had built an API for it, right?

268
00:28:00,560 --> 00:28:01,040
Or were you?

269
00:28:01,360 --> 00:28:01,760
Yeah, okay.

270
00:28:01,920 --> 00:28:02,000
Yeah.

271
00:28:02,160 --> 00:28:18,400
So they said there was an API in Core Motion for this, but it was, I mean, it was the strangest couple of weeks of my life where I saw this, like there was just like one slide at, why one slide at WWDC that like Phil Schiller said, hey, there's this cool motion coprocessor that, you know, does step counting and he moved on.

272
00:28:18,480 --> 00:28:20,560
It didn't really, it wasn't a main feature of the five S.

273
00:28:20,720 --> 00:28:22,160
It was just a thing that they talked about.

274
00:28:22,720 --> 00:28:36,800
And then, you know, they released the Goldmaster SDK for iOS that, you know, that afternoon and there was no documentation for this anywhere, but I like look in the core motion header files and like there is, there's all the APIs for doing this in, you know, core motion.

275
00:28:37,760 --> 00:28:43,680
And so I have no, there's the simulator can't, you know, has no way of simulating steps as you would imagine.

276
00:28:43,840 --> 00:28:52,560
So I like built, I built, I built an entirely an app that week, entirely speculative based.ed on my guesses based on how these headers would work in practice.

277
00:28:52,960 --> 00:29:04,640
And I, you know, sort of built the app and it went and camped outside my local Apple store on iPhone launch day so that I could get an iPhone as soon as they opened.

278
00:29:04,800 --> 00:29:13,360
You know, I got my iPhone at like, you know, that store, Apple store opened at 10 a.m., I got my iPhone at, you know, 10, 10, 10, 15, ran back to my office, plugged it in, like tried the thing that worked.

279
00:29:13,440 --> 00:29:23,840
It didn't quite work, but, you know, within a few hours I was able to get a working version of the app and submitted that and, you know, was able to be as a result kind of like the first person to have an app that did this.

280
00:29:24,160 --> 00:29:34,400
And that meant that, you know, Pinometer just became, it took off and did well in a way that I'd never had any of my previous apps get there.

281
00:29:34,720 --> 00:29:38,960
And so health and fitness then became kind of my main focus for a while.

282
00:29:39,120 --> 00:29:40,640
And I launched a bunch of different apps.

283
00:29:40,800 --> 00:29:44,880
And then relatively shortly thereafter, Apple launched the Apple Watch.

284
00:29:45,040 --> 00:29:51,840
And that became even more of an obvious place where I had this expertise and a bit of a brand around health and fitness.

285
00:29:52,240 --> 00:29:57,680
And so turning that into watch apps where it's all about health and fitness, you know, was an obvious step.

286
00:29:57,920 --> 00:30:05,920
And so like I launched a sleep tracker and a workout tracker and an activity app and it became kind of a thing that worked well for me.

287
00:30:06,000 --> 00:30:27,040
And it was sort of it worked well from a business perspective as well as also I found very quickly that it's one of the most rewarding kinds of work I've ever done where to feel like you're actually doing things that have a tangible benefit to someone else's life in a way that as much as it's like fun when I made an audiobook app that hear people say, Oh, I love your app.

288
00:30:27,280 --> 00:30:27,600
It's cool.

289
00:30:27,760 --> 00:30:28,720
I listen to audiobooks.

290
00:30:29,200 --> 00:30:36,320
It's a very different thing entirely when you hear about someone who is recovering from an injury and uses your app to be able to walk again.

291
00:31:12,080 --> 00:31:24,160
You've talked about this a lot in your show, which everybody who's an iOS developer should definitely go listen to under the radar and maybe just listen to the whole back catalog because honestly it's like a book of just knowledge being dumped in your brain.

292
00:31:24,400 --> 00:31:36,960
But one thing that you kind of touched on at the beginning there was this idea that you'd go to WWDC and you'd look for new like APIs or concepts.

293
00:31:37,360 --> 00:31:45,360
And then in this case, you very quickly built an app around it and you were able to leverage that to be kind of the first one on the store to properly use this API.

294
00:31:46,880 --> 00:31:52,880
Was that a strategy that came out of the experience of Pedometer Plus Plus or were you already doing that for years before that?

295
00:31:54,080 --> 00:31:55,040
I was doing that for years.

296
00:31:55,440 --> 00:32:08,000
I feel like that was, and I think it comes from those early days in the App Store where there were so few apps that if you were the first one to do something, you had a better, a reasonable chance of being successful.

297
00:32:08,240 --> 00:32:16,160
And I think that, like, for better or worse, sort of engendered in me this belief that being there on day one is super important.

298
00:32:16,560 --> 00:32:23,360
And I think it's much less important now than it was in the early days, but it still certainly is a valuable thing.

299
00:32:23,520 --> 00:32:24,960
And it is a situation that is very important.

300
00:32:24,960 --> 00:32:49,200
It is a situation that time and time again has allowed me some edge over bigger companies, I think, or bigger development shops or whatever you kind of want to imagine that my ability, like one of the few things I have as one developer versus a team of ten developers is I can move more quickly because I can't do as much as a team of ten developers, but I can make a decision and run with it.

301
00:32:49,680 --> 00:32:51,520
And I don't have to communicate with someone else.

302
00:32:51,600 --> 00:32:52,560
I don't have to get approval.

303
00:32:52,720 --> 00:33:00,480
We don't need to have like a sprint planning meeting and then every two weeks have our sprint reviews and all these things that you need to do.

304
00:33:36,320 --> 00:33:42,160
And so I think it's a different situation for me and that's something that those early years kind of gendered.

305
00:33:42,320 --> 00:33:46,080
And then I found, you know, several times being there early has been helpful.

306
00:33:46,480 --> 00:33:49,200
You know, certainly with Widget Smith, that was absolutely the case.

307
00:33:50,080 --> 00:33:58,160
And so it's, it's like, it's, I don't know, maybe it's just a bad, it's in some ways it's almost like a bad habit that just keeps being reinforced, but time and time again.

308
00:33:58,720 --> 00:34:10,240
Yeah, like every year when the new version of iOS drops and everybody's kind of scrambling, there's always there's always the people who are calm because they're not scrambling and they're kind of making fun of everyone for scrambling.

309
00:34:10,480 --> 00:34:10,640
Sure.

310
00:34:10,960 --> 00:34:20,880
And it's, it's probably true that for for 80% of people scrambling it doesn't matter, but the 20% for who it does matter don't know who they are necessarily.

311
00:34:21,120 --> 00:34:29,120
And so there's a little bit of a lottery aspect to it, but the more tickets you buy in the lottery, the higher your chances, something like that.

312
00:34:29,840 --> 00:34:30,320
Something like that.

313
00:34:30,480 --> 00:34:33,600
Because at the same time, like I had day one iMessage apps.

314
00:34:33,680 --> 00:34:33,760
Yeah.

315
00:34:34,160 --> 00:34:38,400
Like I launched like five iMessage apps on day one in the iMessage app store.

316
00:34:38,880 --> 00:34:40,000
That was not a sound investment.

317
00:34:40,240 --> 00:34:42,000
Like that time I will never get back.

318
00:34:42,400 --> 00:34:48,960
But the thing, the thing that's interesting though is you've built up even among indie developers a unique skill of listening.

319
00:34:48,960 --> 00:34:49,760
of launching apps.

320
00:34:50,240 --> 00:34:59,920
Like that alone, being able to turn something around quickly and turn something around of a high quality quickly is something that I feel like a lot of people don't have.

321
00:35:00,320 --> 00:35:09,760
And I assume you're very good at either copying and pasting stuff from other apps so that you can quickly build out your setting screens and all that, or you have libraries.

322
00:35:11,040 --> 00:35:24,160
But either way, like you've built up this ability to see a concept, build an idea or an app around it, and then very quickly execute on that and then feel the market out and see, is that a thing that will work?

323
00:35:24,800 --> 00:35:32,880
And I don't think that's something that you would necessarily have been able to do for Widgetsmith, for example, had you not done this, like you said, like 50 other times.

324
00:35:33,360 --> 00:35:33,440
Yeah.

325
00:35:33,760 --> 00:35:34,960
No, and I think that's very true.

326
00:35:35,120 --> 00:35:35,840
And I don't know.

327
00:35:36,560 --> 00:35:41,760
I think there is certainly an element of just my personality that I'm able to.

328
00:35:45,200 --> 00:35:51,440
I don't feel like I think it is a common challenge in this community to want everything to be 100% perfect.

329
00:35:52,320 --> 00:35:58,000
To want, like, version one to be amazing, like, to be just incredibly finely tuned and polished.

330
00:35:58,240 --> 00:36:00,880
And that is in many ways the ethos of the, you know, the Apple community.

331
00:36:01,440 --> 00:36:01,840
Oh, yeah.

332
00:36:02,400 --> 00:36:10,800
And I think there's a tension though between, obviously, it's like, you can have that desire, but you either, you have to be able to actually ship the app at the end of the day.

333
00:36:11,520 --> 00:36:26,240
And I think I have a reason, I think I have enough of a, it's like, clear-mindedness that I don't think I'm capable of shipping the apps that are, like, worth that level of polish.

334
00:36:26,560 --> 00:36:37,440
That I can't achieve the smoothness in animation or coolness of graphic design of some of the apps where it might make sense or be a Um, appropriate for that.

335
00:36:38,080 --> 00:36:42,000
I think I have a reasonably good understanding of like what I can do and what I can't do.

336
00:36:43,040 --> 00:36:46,880
And I think that has helped me to be able to say like, okay, this is good enough.

337
00:36:47,280 --> 00:36:49,120
This is as good as I'm going to be able to get it.

338
00:36:49,360 --> 00:37:03,680
And then, you know, certainly to your point, like it helps that I've built enough apps that, you know, when I'm building a setting screen or when I'm doing, um, you know, watch connectivity or there's a lot of like, there's a lot of weird tar pits that I could get stuck in, um, in iOS development that I like.

339
00:37:04,000 --> 00:37:13,520
I have my little path, you know, elevated pathway over that tar pit that I've built, um, because I've done it, because I've built an app that failed, but I had to go down that road once before.

340
00:37:13,840 --> 00:37:14,000
Right.

341
00:37:14,160 --> 00:37:23,760
And so I have a big breadth of code that I can draw from and not have to solve, you know, like there's a lot, there's so many different problems that you have to solve when you make an app.

342
00:37:24,160 --> 00:37:31,840
And many of those problems I've already solved once before, or at least solved close enough to what I'm doing now that I can then repeat it.

343
00:37:31,920 --> 00:37:40,480
You know, there's always the new stuff, like, you know, building widgets, it's like, it's a whole new thing, and it's Swift UI, and it's a totally new technology that hadn't existed before.

344
